    <%# == Deliveries the endpoint REFUSED
        Every other panel on this page is built out of runs that were ACCEPTED. This one is the
        only surface in the product that can say a delivery arrived and was thrown away — and
        before it existed, that event left no platform-side record at all: `render_bad_request`
        renders JSON and returns, and nothing was written.

        The reason that mattered is one line up the page. `authenticate_api_key!` stamps
        `api_keys.last_used_at` on the way IN, so a refused delivery still records a use, and the
        Connect panel's "Connection" stat reads exactly that column. A repository whose every run
        was being refused therefore rendered `Connected`, in success tone, with a fresh "last
        request" hint — three affirmative healthy signals over a pipeline that was 100% failing.
        The stat now consults `@rejected_ingests` too; this panel is the detail behind that verdict.

        Rendered ALWAYS, including when there is nothing to list, and not gated on
        `@latest_test_run` the way the per-example panels are. Two reasons, and neither is symmetry
        with the siblings:

        * A repository that has never had a run accepted is not the empty case here — it is the
          case where every delivery it ever made was refused, which is exactly when the reader
          needs this list.
        * "No delivery has been refused" is a POSITIVE finding, and it is the one this page could
          not previously make. The Connection stat above now depends on these rows, so a panel that
          vanished when the news was good would leave the reader unable to tell "nothing was
          refused" from "SpecGuard does not track that". No other panel on this page says anything
          about refusals, so this empty state is not a milder restatement of one that already
          exists.

        Outside the `manage_keys` gate, like the Overview, Suite growth and Recent runs: this is
        read-only delivery telemetry. Nothing credential is on these rows — a refusal is recorded
        only for a request that already AUTHENTICATED, so the key is not what is at fault and the
        panel names no key. %>
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Rejected deliveries", id: "rejected-ingests",
                                      class: "lg:col-span-3") do %>
      <% if @rejected_ingests.any? %>
        <%# What these rows ARE, stated before them rather than left to be inferred. Three claims
            here are ones a reader would otherwise guess wrong, and each is guessed wrong in a way
            that costs them something:

            1. These are NOT failed logins. A wrong or revoked key gets a 401, which resolves no
               repository and so cannot be attributed to one — `IngestRejection` argues that at
               length. A reader who took this for "everything that failed" would read an empty
               panel as proof their key is fine.
            2. Nothing here is recoverable. The payload was refused and is not stored, so this is
               a record that it happened and what the endpoint said — not a retry queue, and
               nothing re-delivers from it.
            3. The list is bounded, so an absent old refusal is not evidence it did not happen. %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="rejected-ingests-basis">
          Deliveries that authenticated successfully and were then refused for their
          <strong>payload</strong> — <code>400 {"error":"bad_request"}</code>. A wrong or revoked
          key gets <code>401</code> instead, which identifies no repository and so cannot be listed
          here; an empty panel is not evidence that no request was rejected for its key.
          These runs were <strong>not stored</strong> and cannot be recovered from this page —
          fix the cause and let CI report again.
          <% if @rejected_ingests.bounded? %>
            Only the most recent <%= IngestRejection::PANEL_LIMIT %> are shown, and SpecGuard
            retains at most <%= IngestRejection::REPOSITORY_RETENTION_ROWS %> per repository, so
            this is a recent window rather than the whole history.
          <% end %>
        </p>

        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["When", "Reported by", "What the endpoint said"],
                                          describedby: "rejected-ingests-basis") do %>
          <% @rejected_ingests.rows.each do |rejection| %>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-3 py-2 whitespace-nowrap align-top text-app-content">
                <%# `title` carries the exact timestamp, because "3 days ago" is the right
                    granularity for reading a list and the wrong one for matching a row against a
                    CI build log. %>
                <span title="<%= rejection.occurred_at.utc.iso8601 %>">
                  <%= time_ago_in_words(rejection.occurred_at) %> ago
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="px-3 py-2 align-top text-app-content-secondary">
                <%# The client's `User-Agent`. This column is the whole reason the header is on the
                    row: `specguard-rspec` sends `specguard-rspec/<version>`, and the concrete
                    failure this panel was built for is a VERSION FLOOR — a gem sending
                    `Content-Encoding: gzip` against an installation deployed before
                    `GzipRequestBody` is refused on every run over 256 KiB, i.e. every large suite.
                    A column of identical old versions beside a column of identical reasons is what
                    turns "we are being refused" into "the old gem is being refused". %>
                <% if rejection.reported_client %>
                  <span class="font-mono text-xs break-all"><%= rejection.reported_client %></span>
                <% else %>
                  <span class="text-app-muted">Not reported</span>
                <% end %>
              </td>
              <td class="px-3 py-2 align-top text-app-content">
                <%# The endpoint's own words, verbatim. `Ingest::Payload` assembled this list and
                    the client was handed exactly it in the `details` of the 400; nothing
                    platform-side re-words it into a verdict here, on the standing rule the ingest
                    path already applies to `outcome`. One reason per line because the client is
                    handed a LIST, and collapsing it into a sentence would invent a delimiter that
                    any error message is free to contain. %>
                <% if rejection.reasons.any? %>
                  <ul class="space-y-1">
                    <% rejection.reasons.each do |reason| %>
                      <li class="font-mono text-xs break-words"><%= reason %></li>
                    <% end %>
                  </ul>
                <% else %>
                  <%# A refusal with no reason recorded. Not synthesised into one: the row says the
                      delivery was refused, and inventing a cause it never carried would be the
                      same class of defect as the stat this panel exists to correct. %>
                  <span class="text-app-muted">No reason was recorded.</span>
                <% end %>
              </td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <%# The good news, said out loud. Deliberately scoped to what this table can actually see:
            "no delivery has been REFUSED FOR ITS PAYLOAD", never "everything is fine". A 401 is
            invisible here by construction, and an empty panel that claimed otherwise would replace
            the false `Connected` this ticket removed with a quieter false claim of its own. %>
        <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
              title: "No rejected deliveries",
              description: "No request has authenticated and then been refused for its payload. " \
                           "Requests rejected for a bad or revoked key are not recorded here — " \
                           "they identify no repository."
            ) %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
